        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 776,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Keeps the diagonal of a matrix and everything above it, and sets everything below it to zero: the upper triangle.\n\nA triangular matrix is half of a symmetric one - a covariance, a correlation, a table of distances between every pair of points - and the half is all the information there is, so np.triu removes the duplicate copy - and, with diagonal 1, the diagonal of self-pairs as well. It is also how masks are made that let each step of a sequence see only the steps after it.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.triu <diagonal: int>\nDefault: 0, keep the main diagonal.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA matrix (or a stack of them). Every input is cut and sent.\n\ndiagonal (int box, also an inlet):\nWhere the triangle's edge is: 0 keeps the main diagonal, 1 moves the edge one diagonal up, -1 one down.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntriangle:\nThe matrix with the other side set to zero.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 to 9 as a 3 x 3 matrix, cut to 1 2 3 / 0 5 6 / 0 0 9. Below, np.triu 1: the diagonal too is cleared with 1. A metro re-sends the matrix five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.tril keeps the other triangle. np.diag keeps only the diagonal. np.where can blank any region given a mask.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Keeps the diagonal of a matrix and everything above it, and sets everything below it to zero: the upper triangle.\n\nA triangular matrix is half of a symmetric one - a covariance, a correlation, a table of distances between every pair of points - and the half is all the information there is, so np.triu removes the duplicate copy - and, with diagonal 1, the diagonal of self-pairs as well. It is also how masks are made that let each step of a sequence see only the steps after it.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.triu <diagonal: int>\nDefault: 0, keep the main diagonal.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA matrix (or a stack of them). Every input is cut and sent.\n\ndiagonal (int box, also an inlet):\nWhere the triangle's edge is: 0 keeps the main diagonal, 1 moves the edge one diagonal up, -1 one down.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntriangle:\nThe matrix with the other side set to zero.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 to 9 as a 3 x 3 matrix, cut to 1 2 3 / 0 5 6 / 0 0 9. Below, np.triu 1: the diagonal too is cleared with 1. A metro re-sends the matrix five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.tril keeps the other triangle. np.diag keeps only the diagonal. np.where can blank any region given a mask."
        },
